+/*CI600: a plain pixel position or size goes straight into the object's coordinates. LVGL keeps them as properties
+ *of a local style, which costs nearly every object a style of its own, 40 bytes or more of the heap. The user
+ *flags say which of x and y, the width and the height the coordinates hold, and lv_obj_get_style_prop() answers
+ *from them through _lv_obj_pos_get(), so layouts and widgets read what the style would have said. A percentage,
+ *LV_SIZE_CONTENT, an alignment or a translation goes through the style as before, and setting one of those turns
+ *what the coordinates hold into properties first, see _lv_obj_pos_prop_set(). What differs from LVGL: a kept
+ *position is where the object is, so a change of the parent's padding or border after the fact doesn't move it,
+ *and another parent sees it where it was*/
+#define DIRECT_POS LV_OBJ_FLAG_USER_1
+#define DIRECT_W LV_OBJ_FLAG_USER_2
+#define DIRECT_H LV_OBJ_FLAG_USER_3
